#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0f1c010-5011-5481-80a3-0cb47f139182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309790} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a5187b-9604-5273-b91a-7be182b0cb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309791} {:answer/id #uuid "ce55d0f9-0f9c-5ff3-bcb6-f447db927598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309792} {:answer/id #uuid "da8005e6-e2e8-5d45-ba27-fd29abaeb9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309793} {:answer/id #uuid "680995e6-a209-5e11-a827-cf8d8c1e6261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce55d0f9-0f9c-5ff3-bcb6-f447db927598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309792} :answer-field/id #uuid "34a2c02c-86e3-5c46-9a01-8a0355ee52d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9530e94-2077-5cc5-bfa5-d3da05501ed7" :question/math-academy-id "q-107060" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-7}-1}{x-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-7}-1}{x-4} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(4)-7}-1}{4-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-7}-1}{x-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-7}-1)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2x-7})}^{2}-{1}^{2}}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{2x-7-1}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{2x-8}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{2(x-4)}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{2x-7}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x-7}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2(4)-7}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f91dc651-1dcb-55c0-ad58-2fb6f060c3e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309797} {:answer/id #uuid "1b842499-2b2e-53b1-82ab-9f617a433e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f91dc651-1dcb-55c0-ad58-2fb6f060c3e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309797} :answer-field/id #uuid "2466bdcc-8923-54b6-89fe-6cd1638dce50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "060847a0-f068-589c-94fd-9b85a0de164c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338477" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5x}}{5-x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5x}}{5-x} & =\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5(5)}}{5-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5x}}{5-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{(5-\\sqrt{5x})(5+\\sqrt{5x})}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{5}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5x})}^{2}}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{25-5x}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5(5-x)}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5\\cancel{(5-x)}}{\\cancel{(5-x)}(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{5x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{5(5)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83e713a7-3de6-53c4-b9e2-14eed83e7e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83e713a7-3de6-53c4-b9e2-14eed83e7e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297014} :answer-field/id #uuid "b39d826d-10a5-561f-a5c0-b456268562a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cea8ecd3-a2e6-51b4-876e-28c17aeaa239" :question/math-academy-id "q-338496" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{x})(2x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{4{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(4x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{\\cancel{(4x-1)}(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x\\cancel{(4x-1)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "573c49ce-e5d8-54dd-897a-3b5674cac952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "573c49ce-e5d8-54dd-897a-3b5674cac952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309801} :answer-field/id #uuid "99721541-de5a-5611-85b3-e921e432021d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25bce61b-a5f1-5031-96bf-625853eef9f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-338504" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}} & =\\frac{3-3}{3(3)-\\sqrt{27(3)}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{(3x-\\sqrt{27x})(3x+\\sqrt{27x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{(3x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{27x})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9{x}^{2}-27x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9x(x-3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9x\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{3x+\\sqrt{27x}}{9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3)+\\sqrt{27(3)}}{9(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+9}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a33edddc-1da0-5d41-a93b-892dcdc07dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076258} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8cefe5-a5a0-5541-932b-e9e5fbe534ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076259} {:answer/id #uuid "df7ddb01-dc80-5476-aa94-7fc2b5a0142e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076260} {:answer/id #uuid "2d727eff-da34-5178-8fa7-980bad27e56b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076261} {:answer/id #uuid "63eb7fd8-208e-52bd-8b1c-8c3561420d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd8cefe5-a5a0-5541-932b-e9e5fbe534ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076259} :answer-field/id #uuid "a542e28e-89c7-5fa7-9efa-480f95f1bcb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "999c69b4-b8e1-5935-8b29-a7c23a4a8300" :question/math-academy-id "q-4463" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0+4}-2}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x+4-4}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
